ce or behind it. The group discussed the need for pictures to illustrate these setups. Alexander confirmed that space is not an issue for either PC or tablet setups. The group discussed user interactions, noting that touchscreens are generally avoided in favor of scanning, with wall-mounted codes used for process changes. Viscosity adjustments are made by scanning and manually changing values, as touchscreens are not user-friendly. The group acknowledged that operators often wear gloves, which can affect touchscreen usability, and noted that screens compatible with gloves are available but not preferred. The same avoidance of touchscreens applies to tablets. Alexander shared his experience with digitalization challenges, noting that he has been working with precast software in Münster for seven years. The group discussed the logistics of raw material and finished goods flow in the Americas, highlighting the central warehouse management in Tutitlan, Greenville, and Windsor, and the differences in Southfield, which receives materials from these warehouses. Elisa explained the concept of centralized laboratories and the potential for integrating multiple stock locations. The group discussed the integration of SAP for future logistics improvements, noting the current use of Excel for tracking purchase orders. The conversation concluded with a discussion on the challenges of warehouse management, particularly in Southfield, and the need for better digital tools to track inventory and avoid expiration dates.\n\nIn the current discussion, the group explored the integration of two systems, WGAM and FormLife, with a focus on incorporating the content of the current system into WGAM. Mike is leading this project, with the group supporting him, though they currently work separately. The rollout process for S4 HANA, which impacts everything, has been postponed, creating uncertainty about the information available. The test trace is running well, but live implementation remains 